            </Main>
            <AtypicalElectricalLoads deIcingCables="false" exteriorLighting="false" hotTub="false" heatedGarage="true" portableAirConditioning="false" pool="false" mixedUse="false" totalUnits="0" energyStarUnits="0" evChargingStation="false" outdoorGasAppliances="false" commonSpace="false" elevator="false" fitnessRoom="false" snowmeltSystem="false" unheatedGarage="false" />
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we identified that your house has excess air leakage associated with the following locations:



** At the connections between the floors, walls, and ceilings



** Around the older wooden windows in the front of the house



** At the wall between your living room and the attic space, which also appears to be less well insulated



** The 5" combustion air supply pipe for the boiler



Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $120 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 4.9 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The above foundation exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 with R20 batt insulation. I recommend you add a minimum R15 insulation to these exterior walls, in conjunction with a future planned upgrade to your exterior siding.



For this upgrade, I modelled adding additional insulation to your exterior walls only. When I was on site we did note a lack of insulation in one wall that is adjacent to the attic space. I will discuss this as well as things to consider if you are planning on bringing the garage into the heated space in the "Additional Comments" part of this report. 



The new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) that was launched on April 1, 2019 can help you cover the cost of the additional insulation. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added to the above foundation exterior walls ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+). As you have an attached garage, the total rebate will be pro-rated based on area of the exterior walls and the area of the walls between the heated space and the attached garage.  



Savings: By adding a minimum R15 to all of the above foundation exterior walls, you could potentially save $470 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 19.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $4,500 in rebates from the DHERP. To qualify for the full $5,000 rebate you would need to also add R15 to the walls between your heated living space and the garage. 



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. My visit on 29 March 2021 satisfies the pre-retrofit requirement. 



The Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program has been extended until March 31 2024, pending available funding, and completion of an exterior insulation retrofit allows to you access additional funding for windows, air sealing, and heating equipment. If you are interested in participating in this program or would like more details about the rebate amount, please contact me for more information. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</mainWalls>
                <windows>

Some of your windows in your house have already been upgraded, while others are older. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these older windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: By replacing these older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $240 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 9.8 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have an on demand oil hot water heater, and I do not recommend placing a high priority on replacing this at this time. However, you did express an interest in getting rid of your oil appliances and switching to propane, so I have modelled the savings for replacing your on demand oil hot water heater with an instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater. 



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, could potentially save $78 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with a new 85% efficient oil boiler. However, as you expressed an interest in switching to propane I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE.



Since your boiler is so new and is already fairly energy efficient, I would not recommend replacing it at this time. I would recommend that you replace the circulation pump with an ECM pump, and I would also recommend that you have a qualified heating contractor investigate your combustion air pipe and ensure both the fresh air supply requirements of your house and the combustion air supply needs of your boiler is satisfied. The combustion air pipe should have an electric damper installed to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when the boiler is operating.



Savings: By replacing your current oil boiler with a 95% AFUE condensing propane boiler, you could potentially save $725 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 25.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). 



While updating your boiler, you should also install ECM circulation pumps and qualify for up to $175 in rebates per pump (up to 2 per fiscal year) from EEIP. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. In addition, LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



You have older T4 light fixtures in both your boiler room and your laundry room. While you may not use these rooms regularly, you could consider replacing these fixtures with LEDs. 



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Fail

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 7.1 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil boiler and your wood stove vent up chimneys and are susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed and operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

        - an HRV

        - a clothes dryer

        - a range hood



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat, which you have already installed.



I also noted when I opened up your HRV that the filters are clogged with dust and debris. I recommend opening up your HRV, vacuuming it out, and washing or replacing the filters every three months, or as necessary. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Insulation - Attic Wall

When examining your living room with the infrared camera, I noted that the wall between the attic and the living room is much colder than the rest of the walls. In the attic, this section of the wall is insulated with rigid board insulation. I recommend that you investigate this further. Once you have confirmed what is in this wall, please reach out to AEA if you have any concerns.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Heated Garage

Your attached, heated garage is not currently included in the heated living space as per the modelling requirements of the EnerGuide program. If you are planning on bringing it in to the heated space, I suggest getting a heating contractor in to confirm whether or not your current boiler will meet the added heating load, and what options you have for zoning. You will also need to to confirm what city requirements there are for permitting. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Yellowknife

The following Dec. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $0.91 per liter 

Propane - $0.608 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.51 per bag, $312.5 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,

Alexandra Giroux, Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: alexandra.giroux@aea.nt.ca

Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, </AdditionalComments>
